{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1497238-chronicles-for-low-generation-fledglings-and-neonates","id":1497238,"title":"Chronicles for Low Generation Fledglings and Neonates","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-11-15T17:01:22-08:00","id":1497238,"title":"Chronicles for Low Generation Fledglings and Neonates","content":"Hello everyone, let's continue to beat that dead horse that I've inflicted elsewhere. I am also sorry if this post feels disjointed as I am having to summarize several ideas without having a Shakespearean monologue.<br /> <br /> Low generation neonates and fledglings are some of my most favorite characters to make and play. The problem being is that I rarely get to play them. Let's have a look at why and reasons why me and other people like them and ways we can play them.<br /> <br /> Some things to nip in the bud before we even begin: We all know there are people want to play these kinds of characters for the power-gaming aspect of that. I am not talking about those people, I am talking about those of us who have a true interest in these kinds of characters and their own personal stories. Diablerists are also a concern, but these characters do not have numbers above their heads naming their generation. A good sire will also caution (or dominate) a new childe into not telling others their generation, if they have even been told at all.<br /> <br /> <br /> Most of my characters are of very or relatively low generation. I have four completed characters built with strong backstories and personalities and they are thus: a 7th generation Toreador neonate sired by a childe of Francois Villon; a 6th or 7th generation Lasombra antitribu neonate, an 8th generation Ventrue neonate/fledgling, a 6th generation Toreador elder, and (hold onto your hats) a 5th generation Malkavian fledgling.<br /> <br /> Each of these characters I've created cover a certain theme in VtM (especially my Malkavian and Toreador) that I enjoy the most. What are these themes? The gothic part of the &quot;gothic-punk&quot; of VtM.<br /> <br /> The interplay between childe and sire, the inheritance of potentially immense and dark power, of being a nigh-eternal outsider and sometimes even existing outside of the traditional culture and power structures of most sects, finding oneself in a portion of immortal society usually reserved for the most ancient and distantly human- I love it all. My two &quot;main&quot; characters are even in romantic relationships with their respective sires, as potentially toxic and abusive as they may be. I'm a fan of gothic romance. I have an ancestress name Jane Eyre; it's in the blood.<br /> <br /> I understand these can be potentially difficult chronicles to run, but I also think they are the most rewarding as they are often what I have read in most vampire literature. There are valid concerns about potential power scaling with elder powers and advanced attributes and abilities. What we don't often consider is how much experience is required to get there and how long that may take. Likewise, the implications of being of such a low generation and having an even more ancient sire raises a lot of questions and concerns themselves. These cases can be resolved by having the sire slightly in the background- involved with the character but not heavy handed or a get out of jail free card. Or the sires could be in a conspiracy themselves with their new childer. Or have them involved to whatever degree you like- I think their relationship is a very important one and deserves to be roleplayed.<br /> <br /> A V20 and older concern is blood pools. Yes, those can be rather massive the lower you go. You get a lot of vitae to spend per turn as well, yes. You can also buff your physical stats to the high heavens. But do you know what they don't tell you? Buffed stats don't guarantee success. And what's worse: having to refill your blood pool back up to a safe level if you've had to spend a lot in a spot of conflict. Certain sires may be willing to share their stocks of blood for their childer, and it's certainly a good thing. Others may make them hunt on their own to build independence. Also a good thing. But what happens when that's not readily an option for you?<br /> <br /> In V5, instead of having elder powers and 6+ dots in anything, we are instead served with a blood potency system that feels most often weaker than what V20 offered. Interestingly, it also comes with its own share of setbacks the more potent once's vitae is. It's almost like a &quot;self-balancing&quot; system. At most, it offers extra dice for buffing and disciplines which aren't guaranteed to succeed. You can also heal extra superficial damage, but that even requires a rouse check, which is a rather large thorn in one's side.<br /> <br /> Before I go further and become even less incoherent and jump from topic to topic, I am going to stop here and hopefully await replies so we can discuss this at length. Maybe some of us can find a potential game as well.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2204-toreador","name":"Toreador","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2204_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"77"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}